 ## Subtopics
 
-### Density-Aware Splitting
-- **What it is:** A complementary step that determines whether to clone or split a primitive based on its gradient score and size.
-- **Difference:** Unlike 3DGS which uses a global scale threshold, this method uses a density-aware criterion suitable for inverse rendering.
-- **When to choose:** When refining primitives based on both reconstruction error and local density.
-- **Link:** [Density-Aware Splitting](3183-density-aware-splitting.md)
+### [Contribution-Based Normalization](16632-contribution-based-normalization.md)
+Normalizes the accumulated photometric gradient by the Gaussian's average rendering contribution across views, rather than the raw number of visible views. It prevents occluded primitives from artificially depressing the gradient score and stalling densification in complex scenes. It builds on [Screen-Space Gradient Densification Criterion](5303-screen-space-gradient-densification-criterion.md) by refining how the gradient signal is scaled.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Linear in the number of views $N$ and primitives [S1, unofficial]. The gradient computation requires a backward pass through the rasterizer and T-matrix for each view [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the gradient of the loss with respect to the T-matrix for each view [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Provides a principled signal for densification that accurately reflects reconstruction error in screen space [S1, unofficial]. Avoids gradient cancellation and projection artifacts [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires access to the T-matrix gradients, which may not be available in all differentiable renderers [S1, unofficial]. The ambiguity in the definition of $z$ may complicate implementation [G].
-- **When to use:** In differentiable volume rendering or inverse rendering settings where accurate densification is critical for high-frequency details [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Linear in the number of views $N$ for each primitive, as it requires accumulating gradients over all views where the primitive contributes.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing gradient accumulators for each primitive.
+- **Strengths:** Avoids the discrepancy introduced by projecting 3D center gradients to the image plane, which conflates depth and lateral motion. Prevents gradient cancellation.
+- **Limitations:** Requires access to the T-matrix backward pass gradients, which may not be available in all rasterizers.
+- **When to use:** When accurate screen-space gradient estimation is critical for densification, especially in scenes with complex geometry where depth and lateral motion are distinct.
+- **When not to use:** When computational resources are limited and the approximation used by 2DGS is deemed sufficient.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the 3DSS (3D Surface Splatting for Inverse Rendering) paper [S1, unofficial]. It addresses limitations in 3DGS [Kerbl et al. 2023] and 2DGS [Huang et al. 2024] by providing a more accurate gradient-based densification criterion [S1, unofficial]. The approach builds on the analytical backward pass of the T-matrix to compute screen-space gradients directly [S1, unofficial].
+Originated in 3D Surface Splatting (3DSS) [Ye et al. 2024b; Yu et al. 2024b], as an improvement over the densification criterion used in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [Kerbl et al. 2023] and the approximation used in 2DGS [Huang et al. 2024]. It addresses the issue of over-reconstructed regions being incorrectly identified for refinement by using a principled criterion based on screen-space gradients [S1, unofficial].
 
